    "q-3751": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3751",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find, in ascending powers of $n,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2-5n)^{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2,$ and $b=-5n.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5n)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(2)^{4}(-5n)^{0}+4(2)^{3}(-5n)^{1}+6(2)^{2}(-5n)^{2}+4(2)(-5n)^{3}+1(2)^{0}(-5n)^{4} \\\\  & =1(16)(1)+4(8)(-5n)+6(4)(25{n}^{2})+4(2)(-125{n}^{3})+1(1)(625{n}^{4}) \\\\  & =16-160n+600{n}^{2}-1\\,000{n}^{3}+625{n}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $n$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n16-160n+600{n}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
