    "q-12484": {
      "dom_id": "step-q12484",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(y+z)^{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=y,$ and $b=z.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(y)^{4}(z)^{0}+4(y)^{3}(z)^{1}+6(y)^{2}(z)^{2}+4(y)^{1}(z)^{3}+1(y)^{0}(z)^{4} \\\\  & =1({y}^{4})(1)+4({y}^{3})(z)+6({y}^{2})({z}^{2})+4(y)({z}^{3})+1(1)({z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+4y{z}^{3}+{z}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $z,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}+4{y}^{3}z+6{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-12484-a",
